 <h2 id="running"><span class="num">14</span>Running it: resilience, security boundary, PHI, logging</h2>
 <ul>
-<li><b>Resilience is timeouts and clear statuses</b>, nothing more. Connect 2 s, read 5 s; a failure is a 503 or 502 within seconds; Onyx retries later. No retry, no circuit breaker, no bulkhead, no degraded mode: MMI is a core service, outages are short, and each of those mechanisms is code that must be tested, configured and explained to Onyx.</li>
+<li><b>Resilience is timeouts and clear statuses</b>, nothing more. Connect 2 s, read 5 s, for MMI and the member information service alike; a failure is a 503 or 502 within seconds; Onyx retries later. No retry, no circuit breaker, no bulkhead, no degraded mode: MMI is a core service, outages are short, and each of those mechanisms is code that must be tested, configured and explained to Onyx.</li>
 <li><b>If this service is down</b>, Onyx passes the EMR's member ID through to the vendor as received. Nothing to build here beyond keeping the service boring: stateless, two replicas, graceful shutdown.</li>
-<li><b>Security is outside this code.</b> Azure API Management in front of the service owns authentication, certificates, credentials and rate limits. The service listens on plain HTTP 9090 inside the network and calls MMI over plain HTTP, as the MMI contract states. Actuator exposes only <code>health</code> and <code>info</code>. Swagger UI and the OpenAPI document are off in <code>PRD</code>. Inside the service, every call must name its caller (<code>clientId</code>, <code>clientType</code>) and carry its own <code>requestId</code>, which comes back in the answer and is on every log line of the call; every response carries <code>Cache-Control: no-store</code> and <code>X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff</code>; error bodies carry neither a stack trace nor an internal URL.</li>
+<li><b>Security is outside this code.</b> Azure API Management in front of the service owns authentication, certificates, credentials and rate limits. The service listens on plain HTTP 9090 inside the network and calls MMI and the member information service over plain HTTP, as the MMI contract states (the member information service's to confirm). Actuator exposes only <code>health</code> and <code>info</code>. Swagger UI and the OpenAPI document are off in <code>PRD</code>. Inside the service, every call must name its caller (<code>clientId</code>, <code>clientType</code>) and carry its own <code>requestId</code>, which comes back in the answer and is on every log line of the call; every response carries <code>Cache-Control: no-store</code> and <code>X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff</code>; error bodies carry neither a stack trace nor an internal URL.</li>
 <li><b>PHI minimisation.</b> Request: caller identification, member ID, date or period of service, optional DOB. Response: identifiers, line of business, the covering coverage period. Never: names, DOB, SSN, MBI, PCP, group names. SSN and names are not even declared on the MMI DTOs. The request and the MMI member DTO override <code>toString()</code> so a date of birth never reaches a log line; the exception handler never echoes Jackson or validation messages. MMI's code and text are forwarded to Onyx: in <code>sourceMessage</code> on NOT_FOUND and in the messages of 400 <code>MEMBER_LOOKUP_REJECTED</code>, 503 <code>HTTP_500</code> and 502 <code>ERROR_MESSAGE</code>; they are not written to the log at INFO.</li>
-<li><b>Logging.</b> One INFO line per request: <code>resolution</code>, <code>clientId</code>, <code>clientType</code>, outcome, reason, the received and resolved IDs, company, line of business, date (and end date), whether defaulted, record count, persons matched, MMI request id, elapsed time. One INFO line per MMI call, with the member id. Member ids are logged in full so support can find every call for a member; dates of birth and names never are. Warnings with a searchable marker for data anomalies (<code>UNREADABLE_SPAN</code>, <code>DUPLICATE_RECORDS_MERGED</code>, <code>COMPANY_INFERRED</code>, <code>VENDOR_PAYER_DEFAULTED</code> (a vendor keys the payer on the company and has no entry for the member's), <code>DOB_UNAVAILABLE_ON_RECORDS</code>, <code>CONVERTED_OVERLAP</code>, <code>CORRELATION_ID_REPLACED</code>, <code>MMI_NOT_FOUND_WITH_MEMBERS</code> when MMI answers 404 but still returns members, which are ignored (the 404 wins); <code>MMI_404_WITHOUT_ENVELOPE</code> when MMI's 404 carries a body that is not MMI's envelope: still NOT_FOUND, but if every id comes back that way check <code>mmi.base-url</code> and <code>mmi.path</code>). The console pattern carries the correlation id and the caller's <code>requestId</code>. <b>Payload logging for troubleshooting:</b> with <code>mmi.log-payloads: true</code> the exact MMI request body and the raw response body (status, content type, elapsed time) are written to the log, unmasked, one line each. It is on in <code>DEV</code> (the stub prints the same lines), <code>FQA</code>, <code>PQA</code> and <code>PQA-LITE</code>, and off in <code>PRD</code>: those bodies contain PHI. A test captures the log during every scenario and asserts that no date of birth on file and no member name ever appears.</li>
