+<li>The member information service: whether it takes every population's id as MMI stores it (its contract shows the THP form, the id, spaces and the two-digit suffix; HPHC and Public Plans ids are sent as stored too); its error contract (a 404 is taken as no records; the 400 and 500 in its contract are assumed); that it needs no authentication, like MMI; that each member entry carries <code>coverageRecords</code> (about five years) when <code>returnCoverageList</code> is true, with plan dates whose date part is the date meant; and the FQA, PQA-LITE and PRD URLs, which follow the PQA naming.</li>
+<li>The line of business: the values sent (<code>SCO</code>, <code>MA-HMO</code>, <code>MA-PPO</code>, <code>D-SNP</code>, <code>MA-TOGETHER</code>, <code>RI-TOGETHER</code>, <code>MA-QHP</code>) are the names Onyx should route on; the coverage record's fields read the ES Members criteria (<code>sourceSysId</code> = <code>sourceSystemId</code>, <code>coverage.subsidiary</code> = <code>subsidiary</code>, <code>coverage.product</code> = <code>productCode</code>); an SCO record carries <code>businessTypeIndicator</code> SH and SCO in its <code>planCode</code>; MA-HMO and MA-PPO records carry SH, <code>subsidiary</code> TAHMO and the <code>businessLineKey</code>; the IPA check against the CRC RTU tables (MA-HMO or MA-PPO becoming an IPA value) is pending, to build with CRC; TMP members are covered by MA-HMO and MA-PPO, all but the IPA variant; and the rules for the other populations (HPHC, commercial) are to come. Until then those members keep MMI's value, so <code>lineOfBusiness</code> carries both kinds of value.</li>
+<li>With <code>dosStartDate</code>, which coverage MMI returns: only the segment covering that date or the whole history; and for a member with no coverage on that date, the member without coverage or a 404 (NOT_FOUND here instead of INACTIVE with its reason). The service evaluates whatever comes back: contiguous segments are still read as one period (<code>coverage</code> dates and <code>coverageId</code>), and a period of service is judged on what comes back for its first day.</li>
 <li>MMI reports the company as <code>THP</code> or <code>HPHC</code>, the values eviCore's payer entries key on. Any other value gets eviCore the default payer and logs <code>VENDOR_PAYER_DEFAULTED</code>: watch for that marker in PQA.</li>
 <li>MMI finds the member from the id as typed, including the 9-, 11- and 14-character forms of a TMP id and ids with hyphens or spaces (for example <code>123456789-01</code>, <code>HP-123456789</code>); the stub assumes the same. The service sends the id unchanged, so if MMI is strict about some form that is a finding to take to the MMI team, not a rule to add here.</li>
 <li>MMI's contract, 200 success, 404 member not found, 400 bad request, 500 internal error, is mapped one to one (section 9.3). To see in PQA: the <code>messages[]</code> each answer carries (the stub's 404 has <code>messageType ERROR</code>, <code>statusCode "404"</code>, <code>messageCode MEMBER_NOT_FOUND</code>; the real values will differ and are forwarded as they come), and that MMI's <code>messageType</code> for an error in a 200 envelope is <code>ERROR</code> (<code>mmi.error-message-types</code>).</li>
 <li>A search by the 9 characters of an HPHC id without the <code>HP</code> prefix does not match HPHC records (affects only what NOT_FOUND means for such an input).</li>
 <li>For converted members, which record carries <code>migrationEffectiveDate</code> and whether the old company's spans are trimmed (affects only the "both records cover the date" branch).</li>
 <li>Whether <code>coverage</code> is null or <code>[]</code> for a member with no spans (both are handled the same way).</li>
-<li>Whether an open-ended coverage record (end date blank or <code>12/31/9999</code>) should count as covering next year. Today it does, so a 2027 date on such a record is ACTIVE; if it should be INACTIVE, the rule to add is a cap, for example the end of the current calendar year.</li>
+<li>Whether an open-ended coverage record (end date blank or <code>12/31/3999</code>) should count as covering next year. Today it does, so a 2027 date on such a record is ACTIVE; if it should be INACTIVE, the rule to add is a cap, for example the end of the current calendar year.</li>
 <li>Onyx's DOB availability for dependent populations (D2); INACTIVE and restricted-member handling (D3, D4); whether Onyx reads bodies on non-2xx (D5); the registered MMI <code>clientId</code> (D7); which callers may use the service (D8).</li>
 </ol>
 
 <h2 id="glossary"><span class="num">20</span>Glossary</h2>
 <dl class="kv">
-  <dt>MMI</dt><dd>Master Member Index: Point32Health's enterprise member service. Internal only; the only thing this service calls.</dd>
+  <dt>MMI</dt><dd>Master Member Index: Point32Health's enterprise member service. Internal only; one of the two services this service calls, with the member information service.</dd>
+  <dt>Member information service</dt><dd>The internal service that returns a member's coverage records (<code>POST /members</code> with <code>returnCoverageList</code>); the line of business is derived from the record covering the date of service (section 9.5).</dd>
   <dt>THP / HPHC</dt><dd>The two heritage companies, Tufts Health Plan and Harvard Pilgrim Health Care. A converted member has one record in each, linked by <code>legacyMemberId</code>.</dd>
   <dt>TMP / SCO</dt><dd>Tufts Medicare Preferred and Senior Care Options: THP Medicare membership. Cards print 9 characters; MMI stores 9 characters + 3 spaces + <code>01</code>. No dependents.</dd>
   <dt>Public Plans</dt><dd>THP's Together, Direct and SNP populations; 11-character continuous IDs; families with dependents.</dd>
@@ -1061,7 +1125,7 @@ member-id:
   <dt>UM vendor</dt><dd>Utilization-management partner: eviCore, Evolent, Carelon, MHK, Optum (which stores the 9-character core only); Onyx itself is also configured as a vendor (11 characters).</dd>
   <dt>Resolved ID</dt><dd>The member ID the lookup resolved to, exactly as MMI holds it for the member it settled on (<code>memberId.resolved</code>).</dd>
   <dt>Vendor id</dt><dd>The resolved ID rendered in one vendor's format, <code>memberId.forVendors[].memberId</code>; the member id that goes on that vendor's request, next to the entry's payer.</dd>
-  <dt>Payer</dt><dd><code>payerId</code> and <code>payerName</code> in each <code>memberId.forVendors</code> entry: the payer Onyx puts on that vendor's request. The vendor's own per company when configured (eviCore: <code>TUFTS</code> for THP, <code>HPHC</code> for HPHC), else the default <code>Point32Health</code>.</dd>
+  <dt>Payer</dt><dd><code>payerId</code> and <code>payerName</code> in each <code>memberId.forVendors</code> entry: the payer Onyx puts on that vendor's request. The vendor's own per company when configured (eviCore: <code>TUFTS</code> for THP, <code>HARVARD PILGRIM HEALTH</code> for HPHC), else the default <code>Point32Health</code>.</dd>
   <dt>Coverage id</dt><dd><code>coverage.coverageId</code>: <code>MEMBER_ID</code> + <code>EFF_DATE</code> + <code>END_DATE</code> run together, letters and digits only, dates <code>yyyyMMdd</code>; names the period behind an answer.</dd>
   <dt>Member resolution</dt><dd>The operation, <code>POST /v1/interop/resolve</code>: the member behind the id, the coverage on the date of service and the id in every vendor's format at once.</dd>
   <dt>Client id, client type</dt><dd>Who is calling: <code>clientId</code> (Onyx sends <code>ONYX</code>) and <code>clientType</code>, <code>EXT</code> for a caller outside the plan, <code>INT</code> for an internal one.</dd>
